      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. it sounds like even if the crime rate isn't actually higher, the sheer \"difference\" in the demographic and cultural make-up of those areas is what makes them feel less secure or \"less polished\" compared to somewhere like Kensington. \n\nso, if i'm looking for a place that's culturally rich and interesting but i don't want to deal with that level of \"social friction\" or unpredictability, would you say it's basically better to just stick to the traditionally white, more affluent parts of london? like, is the trade-off for diversity essentially a trade-off with safety and predictability for a solo traveler?",
